[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## C behaviours that had to be replicated exactly
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Each of these is a place a naive translation diverges. All were checked
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m against the C binary and all match.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 1. **`scanf` ignores its return value.** `main` never checks whether the two
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    conversions succeeded, so a failed conversion leaves the local at its `0`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    initialiser rather than producing an error. `abc`, `-`, `+`, `- 5`, `.`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `0x10`, empty input and whitespace-only input therefore all print **nothing**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    and exit **0**. Verified: `first_field_not_a_number`, `sign_with_no_digits`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `whitespace_only_input`, `empty_input`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 2. **A failed first conversion suppresses the second.** `scanf` stops at the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    first failing directive, so `y` is never even attempted. Both fields stay 0.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 3. **`%d` skips leading whitespace and reads across newlines.** `3\n2`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `5\r\n3`, `\x0b5\x0c3` and `\t\t3\t\t2\t\t` all convert two fields — the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    whitespace set is `isspace`'s, i.e. space, `\t`, `\n`, `\v`, `\f`, `\r`. The
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    Rust `Scanner::is_space` matches exactly this set. Verified:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `scanf_reads_across_newlines_and_odd_whitespace`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 4. **The literal space in `"%d %d"` may match an empty whitespace run.** `0-3`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    converts `x = 0, y = -3`; there is no requirement that a separator be
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    present. Verified: `explicit_signs_and_leading_zeros`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 5. **`%d` is base 10 and stops at the first non-digit.** `0x10` converts `0`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    and then fails on `x`; `5e3 2` converts `5` and then fails on `e`, so `y`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    stays 0 (not 2). Verified: `hex_and_exponent_are_not_special_for_percent_d`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 6. **A sign with no digit behind it is a matching failure, not a zero.**
